{"step_index":7141,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T15:01:54Z","thinking":"The error message \"storageQuotaExceeded\" immediately points to a quota issue with the service account. This aligns perfectly with the user's critique and fourth paragraph, which likely mentioned storage limitations. The suggested solutions involve either using shared drives or OAuth delegation to circumvent the quota. This storage constraint is the likely root cause.\n\n\nIt seems the primary issue with Google Drive is that Service Accounts lack their own storage quota when creating files in \"My Drive,\" leading to ownership complexities. The documentation reinforces this, suggesting shared drives or OAuth delegation as workarounds. To achieve the backup goals, shared drives appear essential to solve the quota issue and potentially facilitate delete-proof behavior.\n\n\nA shared drive with a service account as a Contributor presents a potential solution: it offers \"upload-only, delete-proof\" behavior due to Contributor permissions. The key question is whether the user has a Google Workspace account that enables Shared Drive creation. OAuth 2.0 with a user account is another option, though it uses the user's quota and lacks a dedicated upload-only scope. The Google Drive API scopes might provide this.\n\n\nInvestigating Google Cloud Storage as a potential \"upload-only\" solution due to existing project resources. The `roles/storage.objectCreator` IAM role provides the desired functionality, according to documentation, explicitly preventing object deletion. Even attempting deletion with other tools should fail due to the enforced permissions.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"Google Drive\" \"Contributor\" delete files \"Shared Drive\"","toolAction":"Searching Shared Drive roles","toolSummary":"Check Shared Drive Contributor role delete permission"}}]}
{"step_index":7142,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T15:02:04Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-25T15:02:04Z\nCompleted At: 2026-09-25T15:02:06Z\nThe search for \"\"Google Drive\" \"Contributor\" delete files \"Shared Drive\"\" returned the following summary:\nIn Google Shared Drives, a user with the **Contributor** role **cannot delete files or move them to the trash**[1][2][3].\n\n### Summary of Contributor Permissions\n*   **What they can do:** Contributors are intended to be content creators; they can add, edit, and update files within the Shared Drive[1][4].\n*   **What they cannot do:** They do not have permission to delete files, move files to the trash, or move files/folders within the Shared Drive[1][5][3].\n*   **Important Limitation:** While Contributors cannot delete files, they **can** often upload a new file with the exact same name as an existing one, which may effectively overwrite or replace the original content without a notification being sent to the owner or admin[6][7][8].\n\n### Deletion Permissions by Role\nIf you need to manage who can delete content, it is helpful to understand the hierarchy of roles in a Shared Drive:\n\n| Role | Can Delete/Trash Files? |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Manager** | Yes[1][9] |\n| **Content Manager** | Yes[1][10][11] |\n| **Contributor** | **No**[1][2] |\n| **Commenter** | No[1][3] |\n| **Viewer** | No[1][3] |\n\nIf you are concerned about accidental deletions, assigning the **Contributor** role (or lower) is the recommended way to restrict those specific privileges[12][10][13].\n\nSources:\n[1] [hiviewsolutions.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEc331M46cWnaX3FqAePCzrrGRR0feKDptAoICNNHtnnkAiUhsPsQ9pqzJMu46bs8BRxbJwkCxVeCiBRyruGx6ub6t25MOmgjg2Gsf0SNi2oxtkkqmZUoU_14blG5pzPaJxKwp2eouCDBMlZBNu4BqIf1OuLkZcblmZQDpQPoFQGjuzsMeZKNP32a4Yj-0wvoFewLXzGKSLI2GSiRxIUlLkRN4=)\n[2] [google.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirec